WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.880
Hi, my name is Ronit
Langer, and I'm

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.310
a junior studying electrical
engineering and computer

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.440
science, and I'm really
interested in problems

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.950
that exist at the boundary of
computer science and biology.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.480
One of the really
pressing problems

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.105
that I'd like to
address in my research

00:00:14.105 --> 00:00:16.410
is the growing opioid crisis
and the effect that it's

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.300
having on our first responders.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.070
When a first responder
comes onto a scene,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.630
they're not sure
what's in the air.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.360
And the problem is, is that
more powerful opioids, when

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.150
they're aerosolized, could
be potentially lethal.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.820
And so what we want to do is
create a biological sensor

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.700
that's able to tell the first
responders whether or not

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.890
those opioids are
present in the air.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.590
The problem is, is that the
current biological sensor we

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.710
have available to us is the
natural mu opioid receptor that

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.030
lives in our brain.

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:43.830
But the mu opioid
receptor actually

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.320
binds promiscuously to
all sorts of opioids,

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.880
leading to the opioid
crisis in the first place.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.930
And so what we want to do, is
to take this mu opioid receptor,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.780
and mutate it in such a way
that it binds very specifically

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.460
to powerful opioids.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.200
The problem is, in
protein engineering,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.660
in general, is that we don't
have a good understanding

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.060
of the relationship between
sequence, structure,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.709
and function of a protein.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.660
Also, the possible
sequence space

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.060
that exists when you
have 20 amino acids

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.790
is so much larger than the
observed amino acid space

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.330
that we see in nature.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.120
And so what we want
to do in this project

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.570
is to be able to take
the mu opioid receptor,

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.460
and mutate it in silica--

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.040
meaning in, on the
computer, and then

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.860
build it in vivo, and
test it in living cells,

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.980
such that we can
speed up this process

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.370
and create more powerful
protein sensors.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.770
And so the way we do this is
we curate a large training

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.850
set of very similar proteins
to the mu opioid receptor,

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.280
and we test to see how
different mutations affect them

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.512
in living proteins.

00:01:42.512 --> 00:01:44.970
And then we use that to train
a machine learning algorithm,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.250
which can then recommend
better sequences for us

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.310
moving forward.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.820
And then we go and we test
those sequences in cells.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.080
And then this process iterates
until we get to the desired

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.920
functionality of our protein.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.340
And so once we've done this
for the mu opioid receptor,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.440
we can take this
technology and extend it

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.750
to many other problems
that exist in the protein

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.448
engineering space.

